  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nToday will begin the widespread triple-digit heat wave across\nthe forecast area. Ridge centered over the Four Corners region\nwill continue to meander in that area over next couple of days,\nbefore pivoting a bit eastward into next week. This will keep us\nfirmly in the grasp of the ridge, resulting in daily very hot\ntemperatures in the High Plains. Given the dry airmass,\ntemperatures are expected to firmly exceed 100 across much of\nthe forecast area through at least Wednesday. The areas that are\nexpected to be the hottest are in western zones and along the\nKS/OK border where NBM probabilities of 105 degrees or greater\nis 40-70%, so there is a solid chance many areas will even be\n105-109. Expect the hottest days of the stretch to be Sunday\nthrough Tuesday. Despite a drier heat and therefore heat indexes\nmay be the same if not even lower than actual temperatures, heat\nheadlines may be needed given the prolonged nature of the event.\n\nAside from the heat, a high based line of convection is \npossible to develop across eastern CO and northwest Kansas and \ntrack into northern counties later this afternoon and early \nevening. If this is to occur, given large dew points depressions\nof nearly 40-50 degrees, strong winds 50-60 mph would be a risk\nwith any convection. Latest 00z HRRR/ARW/NSSL have come in \ndecent agreement of this scenario occurring, so some POPs were \nadded. SPC Marginal Risk has been expanded to include more of\nthe forecast area given the recent trends. Overall rainfall is \nexpected to be minimal given the dry air and some of that may \nstruggle to reach the ground and appear as virga. These \nconditions could also result in lightning igniting any dry \nvegetation. Though recent rains in some spots could limit some \nof that risk. Sunday could see a similar situation, with some \nshort term guidance suggested some convection in eastern CO that\ntries to get into western zones, but coverage looks to be even \nmore sparse than today. If any storms were to occur, again, dry\nlightning leading to grassland fires could be a concern. Fire \nweather headlines were considered to highlight the risk, but \ncollaboration concluded to hold off until greater confidence.\n\nThe hot and dry spell looks to come to an end by mid week next \nweek as a pattern flip is expected. Long term NBM and various \nglobal models continue to show multiple rounds of widespread \nrainfall each evening/night from Wednesday evening and into the\nweekend. Forecasting rainfall with such confidence for the area\nthis far out is always a challenge and garners quite a bit of\nattention. It should be noted that day to day variability will \nexist and some areas may make out better than others when \neverything is said and done, but the pattern does appear \nfavorable for widespread welcoming rainfall. Current POPs in the\nforecast area each evening and night during the stretch are \n30-70%. Specifics on totals and severe weather potential is \nminimal now, but daily details will become more evident as we \nget closer.",
